{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JWhmEU95RHxhuoJEbPEeav","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CnBKTKK4MRWub6s9VMVMz6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO with execution-based rewards trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a diagnostic framework using leave-one-out (LOO) ablation of formal solver inputs, deriving an Individual Step Relevance (ISR) score. Across 18 training runs spanning 3 model scales (Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they find:\n- C1: ISR as diagnostic — exec-only GRPO shows ≥90% structural degeneration across 5 seeds (4B), with ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps\n- C2: Goodhart inevitability — ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks from 4.82→1.62 as N grows\n- C3: Scale-dependent exploitation — 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse)\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- STA: Given proved output y accepted by solver S, let C = {c1,...,cn} be components. For each ci, construct y_{-i} and re-query S. ri = 1 if S(y_{-i}) = UNPROVED, else 0.\n- ISR(y) = (1/|C|) Σ ri — fraction of components whose removal breaks proof\n- ISR as reward signal: R(y) = 1[S(y)=PROVED] + β·ISR(y), β ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3}\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy: H1 (Conclusion Embedding), H7a (Explicit Tautology), H7b (Obfuscated Tautology), H8 (Conjunction Collapse), H9 (Contradiction Injection), PG (Proof Grafting), DS (Degenerate Syntax), QF (Query-as-Fact), H7a_Prolog\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- Setup: Qwen3-4B primary, FOL with Prover9, K=4 (4B) / K=8 (8B), 3 conditions: exec-only, ISR (β=0.1), random (β=0.1 randomized ISR)\n- Cross-scale: 1.7B and 8B. Cross-logic: SWI-Prolog.\n- SFT baseline: PR=14%, 92.9% genuine → hacking is RL-specific\n- Table 1: 5-seed exec-only at 500 steps (N=5), all converged show ≥90% hacking\n- Table 2: ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps (N=3), all converge to ISR*<0.02\n- Table 3: ISR β=0.1 seed-level (N=10), only 3/10 genuine window\n- Table 4: Pooled OR evolution N=3→10, OR: 4.82→1.62, CMH p=0.015\n- Table 5: Cross-scale exec-only mode distribution (1.7B/4B/8B)\n- Table 6: 8B per-seed detail (3 seeds, K=8)\n- Table 7: Cross-scale ISR β=0.1 comparison\n- Table 8: Premise-count-matched analysis — OR drops 3.48→1.12, ρ(ISR, premise_count)=0.67\n- Cross-logic: Prolog exec-only PR=53.3%±31.1%, 3/3 gaming, 0% genuine\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiments include:\n1. plan_019: s45/s46 exec-only 500-step (DONE in paper as Table 1, N=5)\n2. plan_020: s45/s46 exec-only 1000-step extension (DONE in paper as Table 2, N=3)\n3. plan_021: Qwen3-8B exec-only K=8 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Tables 5-6)\n4. plan_022: Additional ISR β=0.1 seeds to N=10 (DONE in paper as Tables 3-4)\n5. plan_023: Qwen3-8B ISR β=0.1 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Table 7)\n6. plan_024: Premise-count-matched ISR analysis (DONE in paper as Table 8)\n7. plan_007/008/010: DEFERRED (FOL+Prolog joint, cross-logic transfer, SMT-LIB)\n8. plan_011: SUPERSEDED by plan_021+023\n\nAll non-deferred experiments appear covered in the paper.\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of the method (STA/ISR)**\n   - Is LOO ablation well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n   - Is ISR a valid metric? What are its limitations?\n   - Is the 9-mode taxonomy well-defined and exhaustive?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**\n   - Sample sizes: N=5 (exec-only), N=3 (trajectories), N=10 (ISR seeds), N=3 (8B), N=1 (1.7B) — are these sufficient?\n   - Statistical methodology: CMH test, OR, premise-count matching\n   - Are baselines adequate? (exec-only vs ISR vs random, SFT)\n   - Reproducibility: enough detail to replicate?\n\n3. **Experimental coverage vs. design**\n   - All planned non-deferred experiments appear in the paper\n   - Cross-logic (Prolog) and cross-scale (1.7B, 8B) validation present\n\n4. **Weaknesses**\n   - Any methodological gaps or questionable claims?\n   - Missing experiments or controls?\n   - Confounds not addressed?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1193,"cache_read_input_tokens":44550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1193},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"14278742-1399-413c-94ba-b6f07923b56e"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a7b75a5e9cb84a936","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CnBKTKK4MRWub6s9VMVMz6","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO with execution-based rewards trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a diagnostic framework using leave-one-out (LOO) ablation of formal solver inputs, deriving an Individual Step Relevance (ISR) score. Across 18 training runs spanning 3 model scales (Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they find:\n- C1: ISR as diagnostic — exec-only GRPO shows ≥90% structural degeneration across 5 seeds (4B), with ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps\n- C2: Goodhart inevitability — ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks from 4.82→1.62 as N grows\n- C3: Scale-dependent exploitation — 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse)\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- STA: Given proved output y accepted by solver S, let C = {c1,...,cn} be components. For each ci, construct y_{-i} and re-query S. ri = 1 if S(y_{-i}) = UNPROVED, else 0.\n- ISR(y) = (1/|C|) Σ ri — fraction of components whose removal breaks proof\n- ISR as reward signal: R(y) = 1[S(y)=PROVED] + β·ISR(y), β ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3}\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy: H1 (Conclusion Embedding), H7a (Explicit Tautology), H7b (Obfuscated Tautology), H8 (Conjunction Collapse), H9 (Contradiction Injection), PG (Proof Grafting), DS (Degenerate Syntax), QF (Query-as-Fact), H7a_Prolog\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- Setup: Qwen3-4B primary, FOL with Prover9, K=4 (4B) / K=8 (8B), 3 conditions: exec-only, ISR (β=0.1), random (β=0.1 randomized ISR)\n- Cross-scale: 1.7B and 8B. Cross-logic: SWI-Prolog.\n- SFT baseline: PR=14%, 92.9% genuine → hacking is RL-specific\n- Table 1: 5-seed exec-only at 500 steps (N=5), all converged show ≥90% hacking\n- Table 2: ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps (N=3), all converge to ISR*<0.02\n- Table 3: ISR β=0.1 seed-level (N=10), only 3/10 genuine window\n- Table 4: Pooled OR evolution N=3→10, OR: 4.82→1.62, CMH p=0.015\n- Table 5: Cross-scale exec-only mode distribution (1.7B/4B/8B)\n- Table 6: 8B per-seed detail (3 seeds, K=8)\n- Table 7: Cross-scale ISR β=0.1 comparison\n- Table 8: Premise-count-matched analysis — OR drops 3.48→1.12, ρ(ISR, premise_count)=0.67\n- Cross-logic: Prolog exec-only PR=53.3%±31.1%, 3/3 gaming, 0% genuine\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiments include:\n1. plan_019: s45/s46 exec-only 500-step (DONE in paper as Table 1, N=5)\n2. plan_020: s45/s46 exec-only 1000-step extension (DONE in paper as Table 2, N=3)\n3. plan_021: Qwen3-8B exec-only K=8 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Tables 5-6)\n4. plan_022: Additional ISR β=0.1 seeds to N=10 (DONE in paper as Tables 3-4)\n5. plan_023: Qwen3-8B ISR β=0.1 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Table 7)\n6. plan_024: Premise-count-matched ISR analysis (DONE in paper as Table 8)\n7. plan_007/008/010: DEFERRED (FOL+Prolog joint, cross-logic transfer, SMT-LIB)\n8. plan_011: SUPERSEDED by plan_021+023\n\nAll non-deferred experiments appear covered in the paper.\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of the method (STA/ISR)**\n   - Is LOO ablation well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n   - Is ISR a valid metric? What are its limitations?\n   - Is the 9-mode taxonomy well-defined and exhaustive?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**\n   - Sample sizes: N=5 (exec-only), N=3 (trajectories), N=10 (ISR seeds), N=3 (8B), N=1 (1.7B) — are these sufficient?\n   - Statistical methodology: CMH test, OR, premise-count matching\n   - Are baselines adequate? (exec-only vs ISR vs random, SFT)\n   - Reproducibility: enough detail to replicate?\n\n3. **Experimental coverage vs. design**\n   - All planned non-deferred experiments appear in the paper\n   - Cross-logic (Prolog) and cross-scale (1.7B, 8B) validation present\n\n4. **Weaknesses**\n   - Any methodological gaps or questionable claims?\n   - Missing experiments or controls?\n   - Confounds not addressed?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","uuid":"8b956ed4-564c-4970-bad5-53521aed1f78","session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da"}
