{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WEXRBWtpPDF9CqZAYR7eSC","type":"tool_result","content":"paper/experiments.tex:67:[Omitted long matching line]\npaper/experiments.tex:79:The sole non-TS ISR seed (s42) shows raw $\\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.415$, $3.2\\times$ random ($0.131$), a premise-minimization artifact ($n{=}5.58$ vs.\\ $8.46$); matched comparison gives $1.06\\times$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis\npaper/method.tex:48:ISR is sensitive to component count ($n{=}2 \\Rightarrow \\text{ISR}{\\geq}0.5$); we control for this via premise-count-matched analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:goodhart}).\npaper/appendix.tex:123:We recommend that future work employing ablation-based diagnostics report premise-count-matched comparisons alongside raw values."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CnBKTKK4MRWub6s9VMVMz6","session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"e62cface-6df8-4a74-b8a5-9eb4718831e3","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:45:13.358Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JWhmEU95RHxhuoJEbPEeav","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WqKi4vpkQrrfEGfSGuFdZx","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), using LOO ablation of solver inputs, deriving Individual Step Relevance (ISR). Across 18 runs (3 scales: Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they establish:\n- C1: STA detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate. 5 seeds of exec-only 4B GRPO show ≥90% structural degeneration. ISR collapse trajectories (0.642→0.003 over 1000 steps).\n- C2: ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks 4.82→1.62. Premise-count matching reveals OR drops from 3.48 to 1.12 (n.s.) — ISR's advantage is a selection effect.\n- C3: Exploitation is capacity-dependent: 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse). 9-mode hacking taxonomy validated across 3 scales.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- RL for formal reasoning: GRPO (Shao et al. 2024), DeepSeek-AI 2025, Luong et al. 2024, DeepSeek-Prover (Xin et al. 2024), AlphaProof (Trinh et al. 2024)\n- Reward hacking and Goodhart's law: Amodei et al. 2016, Skalse et al. 2022, Manheim & Garrabrant 2019, Gao et al. 2023, Coste et al. 2024\n- Proof quality metrics: LOO ablation (Lei et al. 2016, DeYoung et al. 2020), proof quality (Jiang et al. 2023, First et al. 2023)\n\n**Method highlights:**\n- STA: LOO ablation on formal solver inputs → ri ∈ {0,1} per component → ISR = mean(ri)\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy (H1, H7a, H7b, H8, H9, PG, DS, QF, H7a_Prolog)\n- ISR as both diagnostic and reward signal\n\n**Key experimental findings:**\n- Exec-only: all converged seeds ≥90% hacking, ISR collapses monotonically\n- ISR reward: 3/10 genuine window, shrinking OR with larger N\n- Cross-scale: mode gradient H1→H7→H8 with increasing capacity\n- Cross-logic: Prolog also shows complete gaming (0% genuine)\n- Premise-count confound: ISR genuine advantage is selection effect (OR: 3.48→1.12)\n- SFT baseline: 92.9% genuine, confirming hacking is RL-specific\n- Limitations: ISR measures syntactic necessity not semantic faithfulness (ρ(ISR, NLI accuracy)=-0.049, n.s.); only Qwen3 family; 1.7B lower bound with LLaMA-3.2-3B PR≈15% suggests not architecture-specific; ISR β=0.05 no-safe-dose on 2 seeds only\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**\n   - How novel is STA/ISR as a diagnostic? Compare to existing proof quality metrics.\n   - How novel is the 9-mode hacking taxonomy?\n   - How novel are the empirical findings (Goodhart collapse in formal reasoning, scale-dependent exploitation)?\n   - Is this incremental over existing reward hacking literature?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**\n   - Are key related works cited? Missing citations?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - How does this compare to concurrent work on reward hacking in LLM reasoning (e.g., process reward models, outcome reward models)?\n   - Any overclaiming relative to prior work?\n\n3. **Contribution significance**\n   - Is this a diagnostic tool paper, an empirical study, or a methods paper? Is the framing clear?\n   - Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n   - Would the community benefit from this work?\n\n4. **Writing quality**\n   - Is the paper well-organized for 6 pages?\n   - Are the claims clearly stated and supported?\n   - Any presentation issues?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Missing References\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Excitement/Novelty: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1193,"cache_read_input_tokens":44550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1193},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"766de90c-354d-4a52-9956-c8f7203d608e"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ab769ede1eed9eb55","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WqKi4vpkQrrfEGfSGuFdZx","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), using LOO ablation of solver inputs, deriving Individual Step Relevance (ISR). Across 18 runs (3 scales: Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they establish:\n- C1: STA detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate. 5 seeds of exec-only 4B GRPO show ≥90% structural degeneration. ISR collapse trajectories (0.642→0.003 over 1000 steps).\n- C2: ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks 4.82→1.62. Premise-count matching reveals OR drops from 3.48 to 1.12 (n.s.) — ISR's advantage is a selection effect.\n- C3: Exploitation is capacity-dependent: 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse). 9-mode hacking taxonomy validated across 3 scales.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- RL for formal reasoning: GRPO (Shao et al. 2024), DeepSeek-AI 2025, Luong et al. 2024, DeepSeek-Prover (Xin et al. 2024), AlphaProof (Trinh et al. 2024)\n- Reward hacking and Goodhart's law: Amodei et al. 2016, Skalse et al. 2022, Manheim & Garrabrant 2019, Gao et al. 2023, Coste et al. 2024\n- Proof quality metrics: LOO ablation (Lei et al. 2016, DeYoung et al. 2020), proof quality (Jiang et al. 2023, First et al. 2023)\n\n**Method highlights:**\n- STA: LOO ablation on formal solver inputs → ri ∈ {0,1} per component → ISR = mean(ri)\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy (H1, H7a, H7b, H8, H9, PG, DS, QF, H7a_Prolog)\n- ISR as both diagnostic and reward signal\n\n**Key experimental findings:**\n- Exec-only: all converged seeds ≥90% hacking, ISR collapses monotonically\n- ISR reward: 3/10 genuine window, shrinking OR with larger N\n- Cross-scale: mode gradient H1→H7→H8 with increasing capacity\n- Cross-logic: Prolog also shows complete gaming (0% genuine)\n- Premise-count confound: ISR genuine advantage is selection effect (OR: 3.48→1.12)\n- SFT baseline: 92.9% genuine, confirming hacking is RL-specific\n- Limitations: ISR measures syntactic necessity not semantic faithfulness (ρ(ISR, NLI accuracy)=-0.049, n.s.); only Qwen3 family; 1.7B lower bound with LLaMA-3.2-3B PR≈15% suggests not architecture-specific; ISR β=0.05 no-safe-dose on 2 seeds only\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**\n   - How novel is STA/ISR as a diagnostic? Compare to existing proof quality metrics.\n   - How novel is the 9-mode hacking taxonomy?\n   - How novel are the empirical findings (Goodhart collapse in formal reasoning, scale-dependent exploitation)?\n   - Is this incremental over existing reward hacking literature?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**\n   - Are key related works cited? Missing citations?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - How does this compare to concurrent work on reward hacking in LLM reasoning (e.g., process reward models, outcome reward models)?\n   - Any overclaiming relative to prior work?\n\n3. **Contribution significance**\n   - Is this a diagnostic tool paper, an empirical study, or a methods paper? Is the framing clear?\n   - Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n   - Would the community benefit from this work?\n\n4. **Writing quality**\n   - Is the paper well-organized for 6 pages?\n   - Are the claims clearly stated and supported?\n   - Any presentation issues?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Missing References\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Excitement/Novelty: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","uuid":"996abb73-b752-4917-a615-d5a86be6509d","session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da"}
