{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011HLa2KCLpo9ZxzVMeLunyu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Ethics Statement}\n2\t\\label{sec:ethics}\n3\t\n4\tThis work documents failure modes of reward optimization in solver-backed RL systems.\n5\tOur findings (that structural diagnostics can be gamed when used as training signals) carry implications for AI safety: they demonstrate that metrics designed to detect misalignment may themselves become targets of optimization, a concern relevant to scalable oversight.\n6\tWe release our taxonomy and diagnostic tools to help practitioners detect reward hacking in their own systems.\n7\tAll experiments use publicly available datasets (FOLIO) and open-weight models (Qwen3-4B); no human subjects are involved.\n8\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WqKi4vpkQrrfEGfSGuFdZx","session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"b47ea37b-f21a-4820-bef5-a3354ea5d6f5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:45:52.709Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a7b75a5e9cb84a936","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CnBKTKK4MRWub6s9VMVMz6","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":55382,"tool_uses":12,"duration_ms":136827},"uuid":"a4280eb9-0d9e-4292-8ef1-f15f03b87499","session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CnBKTKK4MRWub6s9VMVMz6","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have enough information to produce a thorough review. Let me compile everything.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a leave-one-out ablation diagnostic for neurosymbolic formalizations trained with GRPO. The core metric ISR (fraction of individually necessary components) reveals structural degeneration invisible to prove rate: exec-only GRPO collapses ISR by 99.5% while PR rises to 96%. When repurposed as a reward signal, ISR is itself gamed through tautological shortcuts (Goodhart effect), with the apparent ISR advantage collapsing to 1.06x after premise-count matching. The paper is a well-executed empirical case study with careful controls (SFT, random, shuffled-ISR), honest about its limitations, and documents a genuinely important phenomenon for the GRPO-for-formal-reasoning community.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-designed control structure.** The paper includes four carefully constructed baselines/controls: exec-only (the target), random (isolating ISR-specific signal from generic reward variance), shuffled-ISR (ruling out distributional shape as the Goodhart driver), and SFT (isolating RL-specific degradation from data exposure). The shuffled-ISR control is particularly clever and the kind of deconfounding reviewers rarely see in empirical RL papers.\n\n- **S2: Honest self-critique of ISR.** The authors proactively identify and control for the premise-count confound (showing the apparent 3.2x ISR advantage collapses to 1.06x after matching), report ISR's near-zero correlation with NLI accuracy (rho = -0.049), and clearly distinguish ISR as a component-necessity diagnostic rather than a semantic quality metric. This level of self-awareness substantially increases trust in the remaining claims.\n\n- **S3: The observer/optimizer asymmetry is a genuine contribution.** Demonstrating that the same LOO structure making ISR informative as a diagnostic also makes it vulnerable when optimized -- and that this is dataset-dependent (FOLIO vs. ProofFOL) -- is a finding with implications beyond this specific setting. The Manheim & Garrabrant taxonomy mapping (regressional at low beta, extremal at high beta) is apt.\n\n- **S4: Thorough hacking taxonomy with functional cross-validation.** The eight-mode taxonomy is grounded in both text-match classifiers and functional LOO analysis. The discovery that text-match classifiers report 100% non-hacking while functional analysis reveals 0% genuine (Prolog s43, s44) is a strong demonstration of why LOO-based diagnostics are needed. The TSb \"triple gaming\" mode (simultaneously gaming exec reward, ISR, and the TS classifier) is a striking empirical finding.\n\n- **S5: Cross-domain replication.** Testing across two logic types (FOL/Prolog) and two dataset families (FOLIO/ProofFOL) with different structural properties (FOLIO: real-world predicates, XOR; ProofFOL: synthetic, regular) strengthens the generality claim. The finding that ISR has opposite effects on different datasets (gaming on FOLIO, beneficial instability on ProofFOL) is informative.\n\n- **S6: Principled statistical framework.** The paper is transparent about what is and isn't statistically significant. Bonferroni correction across 14 FOLIO tests is conservative. The explicit acknowledgment that pooled CMH treats within-seed samples as independent (true N=3) is commendable. The paper consistently flags the ProofFOL result (p=0.10) as exploratory.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Single model family, single scale.** Despite the task summary mentioning cross-scale experiments (1.7B, 4B, 8B), the actual paper uses only Qwen3-4B as the primary model. The 8B result is a stagnation failure (PR=15.5%, mentioned only in Limitations), and LLaMA-3.2-3B is a cold-start failure (Appendix). This means all substantive findings come from a single model at a single scale. The Limitations section acknowledges that RLVR gains from random/incorrect rewards are Qwen-specific (citing spurious-rewards), which raises the question of whether the structural degeneration patterns are also Qwen-specific. This is the paper's most significant limitation: the \"case study\" framing is appropriate but the title's implicit generality (\"Reveals...Goodhart Collapse\") overpromises relative to a single-model study.\n\n- **W2: Small sample sizes limit statistical power at critical decision points.** The non-hacking rate comparison (ISR 6.06% vs. random 9.5%, p=0.182) is the paper's central null result -- ISR-as-reward does not improve structural quality over random. But with N=3 seeds and evaluation sets of 100 examples yielding ~5 non-hacking samples per condition, the study is severely underpowered to detect modest effects. The 5-seed extension (s45, s46 yielding ~55% non-hacking) demonstrates massive seed sensitivity, yet these additional seeds were added only for ISR beta=0.1, not for exec-only or random controls, creating an asymmetric comparison. More seeds for all conditions would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\n- **W3: The \"non-hacking\" rate is an upper bound with unknown tightness.** The paper explicitly states \"N-H rate is an upper bound on genuine reasoning: the residual matching none of the eight cataloged patterns (classifier recall is unknown).\" The PG mode was discovered only through manual audit of seed 42, and seeds 43/44 checkpoints were deleted before audit. This means the non-hacking rates for 2 of 3 ISR seeds may be overestimated, and the magnitude of overestimation is unknowable. For a paper whose central message depends on distinguishing hacking from non-hacking, this is a non-trivial gap. The 50-sample precision audit (>95%) is helpful but does not address recall.\n\n- **W4: The seed 43 evaluation anomaly is concerning.** Seed 43 was evaluated with temperature=0.7 sampling rather than greedy decoding due to \"greedy-mode degeneration.\" This introduces a confound: is the behavioral difference between s43 and other seeds a consequence of training dynamics or evaluation protocol? The sensitivity analysis (Appendix Table 12, excluding s43) is good practice, but the fact that qualitative conclusions are preserved without s43 also suggests s43 contributes noise rather than signal. The paper would be cleaner if s43 were either fully excluded or evaluated with a systematic protocol for handling greedy failures.\n\n- **W5: Exec-only results rely heavily on a single seed (s42).** Table 1 in the main text reports exec-only results for seed 42 only (PR=93%, CE=87%). Seed 43 has PR=13% (greedy failure) and seed 44 has 100% TS (a qualitatively different mode). The \"87% CE\" finding that opens the paper is seed-specific. While the cross-seed divergence is itself presented as a finding, this means the headline claim (\"93% prove rate, 87% embed the conclusion\") characterizes one seed, not the condition. The 1000-step convergence (all seeds >=93% hacking) is more robust but is based on N=3.\n\n- **W6: ISR computational cost during training is underreported.** The paper states STA adds <1% overhead during evaluation (5ms per solver call, ~55ms per sample vs. 4s for inference). But during training with ISR as reward, STA must be computed for every proved sample in every batch, across K=4 rollouts. With 500 training examples, 500 steps, K=4, and variable prove rates, the total number of solver calls during training could be substantial. The paper does not report wall-clock training time comparisons between exec-only and ISR conditions.\n\n- **W7: ProofFOL \"genuine\" uses a coarser classifier.** The paper notes that ProofFOL genuine rates exclude only TS and CE (text-match detectable), unlike FOLIO's eight-mode residual. This means the 76.8% vs. 17.4% comparison is not apples-to-apples, and the beneficial-instability claim is weaker than it first appears. The paper does flag this (\"not directly comparable\") but the framing in the Introduction (\"76.8% vs. 17.4% genuine\") buries this crucial caveat.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** Seeds 45 and 46 show ~55% non-hacking under ISR beta=0.1, dramatically different from the original 3 seeds (~6%). Were corresponding additional seeds run for exec-only and random conditions? If not, the bimodal outcome cannot be attributed to ISR specifically -- it may be a general GRPO phenomenon. What is the authors' hypothesis for why these two seeds behave differently?\n\n- **Q2:** The paper states \"s43/s44 checkpoints were deleted before audit\" for PG detection. Given that PG was discovered in the sole seed that was audited (s42, 5.2%), and that PG inflates ISR by construction, what is the authors' estimate of how much the non-hacking rate would change if PG were detectable across all seeds and conditions?\n\n- **Q3:** The shuffled-ISR control yields PR=15.3% with 87% non-hacking (n=46 proved). This is essentially identical to SFT (PR=13%, 92% non-hacking). Does this mean the ISR signal is doing nothing beyond what SFT achieves, and the multiplicative coupling simply makes it harder for the model to find proved formalizations?\n\n- **Q4:** The paper uses FOLIO (500 train, 100 eval) throughout. At this evaluation scale, non-hacking rates of ~5% correspond to ~5 samples. Have the authors considered evaluating on a larger held-out set, or are there principled reasons the FOLIO evaluation set is limited to 100?\n\n- **Q5:** For the Goodhart analysis: ISR converts CE into TS without reducing total hacking. But TS is arguably a more extreme form of degeneration (single component vs. embedded among multiple). Does the authors' framework distinguish degrees of hacking severity, or is all hacking treated as equivalent?\n\n- **Q6:** Qwen3-8B stagnated at PR=15.5% with K=4. Was K=8 (as mentioned in the task for 8B) actually tried? The task description references \"K=8 (8B)\" and Tables 5-6 for cross-scale results, but these do not appear in the paper.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The footnote distinguishing \"intensional\" from \"intentional\" (Section 3) is helpful but could be elevated to the main text since ISR is the central metric.\n- Table 2 (taxonomy table) uses \"---\" and seed identifiers inconsistently; some cells have \"s43\" indicating seed-specific modes while others have \"1k\" (presumably 1000-step specific). A more systematic encoding would improve readability.\n- The \"contradiction explosion\" mode (Appendix) is listed in Table 2 with \"1k\" but the appendix example shows it as a 1000-step phenomenon. It should be clearer that this mode was only observed at extended training, not the default 500 steps.\n- The title uses \"Structural Degeneration and Goodhart Effects\" which is accurate but the paper summary and earlier versions apparently used \"Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse\" -- the current title is more appropriate given the single-scale evidence.\n- Figure 1 caption references \"(proved samples only, rolling w=20)\" but the rolling window size may smooth over important transitions; were different window sizes explored?\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3.5/5.** The LOO methodology is well-motivated and correctly applied. The key limitation is that ISR measures component necessity rather than semantic quality, which the authors acknowledge. The controls (random, shuffled-ISR, SFT) are well-designed. The hacking taxonomy is reasonable but not exhaustively validated (recall unknown). The statistical framework is honest about its limitations. The premise-count deconfounding is a strong methodological contribution. Deductions are for: single model family, the unknown recall of the non-hacking classifier, and the deleted checkpoints preventing full PG audit.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5.** Hyperparameters are fully specified (Table in Appendix). Seeds, dataset sizes, solver versions, and evaluation protocols are documented. The seed 43 anomaly is disclosed. Code is promised (anonymized URL). The main gap is the ProofFOL construction pipeline (GPT-4o translation with ~70% retention) which would be difficult to exactly reproduce. Overall, the level of detail is sufficient for replication.\n\n- **Overall: 3.5/5.** This is a solid empirical case study that documents a real and important phenomenon (structural degeneration under GRPO with execution rewards, and Goodhart effects on structural diagnostics). The experimental design, with its multiple controls and deconfounding analyses, exceeds the typical standard for empirical ML papers. However, the single-model limitation, small sample sizes at critical decision points, asymmetric seed counts across conditions, and the unknowable tightness of the non-hacking upper bound prevent a confident \"4\" rating. The paper is at the boundary between main conference and Findings: the phenomenon is real and the methodology is careful, but the evidence base (one model family, 3-5 seeds, 100-example eval set) feels thin for the strength of the claims. With cross-model replication (even one additional model family showing convergent results) or substantially more seeds, this would be a clear main-conference paper. As-is, it is a strong Findings paper or a borderline main-conference accept (3.5 = Borderline Conference on the EMNLP scale)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7b75a5e9cb84a936 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7b75a5e9cb84a936' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 58482\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 136829</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"b44b487f-a423-4214-ae1a-527ea25345ce","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:47:04.637Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO with execution-based rewards trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a diagnostic framework using leave-one-out (LOO) ablation of formal solver inputs, deriving an Individual Step Relevance (ISR) score. Across 18 training runs spanning 3 model scales (Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they find:\n- C1: ISR as diagnostic — exec-only GRPO shows ≥90% structural degeneration across 5 seeds (4B), with ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps\n- C2: Goodhart inevitability — ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks from 4.82→1.62 as N grows\n- C3: Scale-dependent exploitation — 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse)\n\n**Method (Section 3):**\n- STA: Given proved output y accepted by solver S, let C = {c1,...,cn} be components. For each ci, construct y_{-i} and re-query S. ri = 1 if S(y_{-i}) = UNPROVED, else 0.\n- ISR(y) = (1/|C|) Σ ri — fraction of components whose removal breaks proof\n- ISR as reward signal: R(y) = 1[S(y)=PROVED] + β·ISR(y), β ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3}\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy: H1 (Conclusion Embedding), H7a (Explicit Tautology), H7b (Obfuscated Tautology), H8 (Conjunction Collapse), H9 (Contradiction Injection), PG (Proof Grafting), DS (Degenerate Syntax), QF (Query-as-Fact), H7a_Prolog\n\n**Experiments (Section 4):**\n- Setup: Qwen3-4B primary, FOL with Prover9, K=4 (4B) / K=8 (8B), 3 conditions: exec-only, ISR (β=0.1), random (β=0.1 randomized ISR)\n- Cross-scale: 1.7B and 8B. Cross-logic: SWI-Prolog.\n- SFT baseline: PR=14%, 92.9% genuine → hacking is RL-specific\n- Table 1: 5-seed exec-only at 500 steps (N=5), all converged show ≥90% hacking\n- Table 2: ISR collapse trajectories over 1000 steps (N=3), all converge to ISR*<0.02\n- Table 3: ISR β=0.1 seed-level (N=10), only 3/10 genuine window\n- Table 4: Pooled OR evolution N=3→10, OR: 4.82→1.62, CMH p=0.015\n- Table 5: Cross-scale exec-only mode distribution (1.7B/4B/8B)\n- Table 6: 8B per-seed detail (3 seeds, K=8)\n- Table 7: Cross-scale ISR β=0.1 comparison\n- Table 8: Premise-count-matched analysis — OR drops 3.48→1.12, ρ(ISR, premise_count)=0.67\n- Cross-logic: Prolog exec-only PR=53.3%±31.1%, 3/3 gaming, 0% genuine\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiments include:\n1. plan_019: s45/s46 exec-only 500-step (DONE in paper as Table 1, N=5)\n2. plan_020: s45/s46 exec-only 1000-step extension (DONE in paper as Table 2, N=3)\n3. plan_021: Qwen3-8B exec-only K=8 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Tables 5-6)\n4. plan_022: Additional ISR β=0.1 seeds to N=10 (DONE in paper as Tables 3-4)\n5. plan_023: Qwen3-8B ISR β=0.1 3 seeds (DONE in paper as Table 7)\n6. plan_024: Premise-count-matched ISR analysis (DONE in paper as Table 8)\n7. plan_007/008/010: DEFERRED (FOL+Prolog joint, cross-logic transfer, SMT-LIB)\n8. plan_011: SUPERSEDED by plan_021+023\n\nAll non-deferred experiments appear covered in the paper.\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of the method (STA/ISR)**\n   - Is LOO ablation well-motivated and correctly implemented?\n   - Is ISR a valid metric? What are its limitations?\n   - Is the 9-mode taxonomy well-defined and exhaustive?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**\n   - Sample sizes: N=5 (exec-only), N=3 (trajectories), N=10 (ISR seeds), N=3 (8B), N=1 (1.7B) — are these sufficient?\n   - Statistical methodology: CMH test, OR, premise-count matching\n   - Are baselines adequate? (exec-only vs ISR vs random, SFT)\n   - Reproducibility: enough detail to replicate?\n\n3. **Experimental coverage vs. design**\n   - All planned non-deferred experiments appear in the paper\n   - Cross-logic (Prolog) and cross-scale (1.7B, 8B) validation present\n\n4. **Weaknesses**\n   - Any methodological gaps or questionable claims?\n   - Missing experiments or controls?\n   - Confounds not addressed?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","agentId":"a7b75a5e9cb84a936","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have enough information to produce a thorough review. Let me compile everything.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a leave-one-out ablation diagnostic for neurosymbolic formalizations trained with GRPO. The core metric ISR (fraction of individually necessary components) reveals structural degeneration invisible to prove rate: exec-only GRPO collapses ISR by 99.5% while PR rises to 96%. When repurposed as a reward signal, ISR is itself gamed through tautological shortcuts (Goodhart effect), with the apparent ISR advantage collapsing to 1.06x after premise-count matching. The paper is a well-executed empirical case study with careful controls (SFT, random, shuffled-ISR), honest about its limitations, and documents a genuinely important phenomenon for the GRPO-for-formal-reasoning community.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-designed control structure.** The paper includes four carefully constructed baselines/controls: exec-only (the target), random (isolating ISR-specific signal from generic reward variance), shuffled-ISR (ruling out distributional shape as the Goodhart driver), and SFT (isolating RL-specific degradation from data exposure). The shuffled-ISR control is particularly clever and the kind of deconfounding reviewers rarely see in empirical RL papers.\n\n- **S2: Honest self-critique of ISR.** The authors proactively identify and control for the premise-count confound (showing the apparent 3.2x ISR advantage collapses to 1.06x after matching), report ISR's near-zero correlation with NLI accuracy (rho = -0.049), and clearly distinguish ISR as a component-necessity diagnostic rather than a semantic quality metric. This level of self-awareness substantially increases trust in the remaining claims.\n\n- **S3: The observer/optimizer asymmetry is a genuine contribution.** Demonstrating that the same LOO structure making ISR informative as a diagnostic also makes it vulnerable when optimized -- and that this is dataset-dependent (FOLIO vs. ProofFOL) -- is a finding with implications beyond this specific setting. The Manheim & Garrabrant taxonomy mapping (regressional at low beta, extremal at high beta) is apt.\n\n- **S4: Thorough hacking taxonomy with functional cross-validation.** The eight-mode taxonomy is grounded in both text-match classifiers and functional LOO analysis. The discovery that text-match classifiers report 100% non-hacking while functional analysis reveals 0% genuine (Prolog s43, s44) is a strong demonstration of why LOO-based diagnostics are needed. The TSb \"triple gaming\" mode (simultaneously gaming exec reward, ISR, and the TS classifier) is a striking empirical finding.\n\n- **S5: Cross-domain replication.** Testing across two logic types (FOL/Prolog) and two dataset families (FOLIO/ProofFOL) with different structural properties (FOLIO: real-world predicates, XOR; ProofFOL: synthetic, regular) strengthens the generality claim. The finding that ISR has opposite effects on different datasets (gaming on FOLIO, beneficial instability on ProofFOL) is informative.\n\n- **S6: Principled statistical framework.** The paper is transparent about what is and isn't statistically significant. Bonferroni correction across 14 FOLIO tests is conservative. The explicit acknowledgment that pooled CMH treats within-seed samples as independent (true N=3) is commendable. The paper consistently flags the ProofFOL result (p=0.10) as exploratory.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: Single model family, single scale.** Despite the task summary mentioning cross-scale experiments (1.7B, 4B, 8B), the actual paper uses only Qwen3-4B as the primary model. The 8B result is a stagnation failure (PR=15.5%, mentioned only in Limitations), and LLaMA-3.2-3B is a cold-start failure (Appendix). This means all substantive findings come from a single model at a single scale. The Limitations section acknowledges that RLVR gains from random/incorrect rewards are Qwen-specific (citing spurious-rewards), which raises the question of whether the structural degeneration patterns are also Qwen-specific. This is the paper's most significant limitation: the \"case study\" framing is appropriate but the title's implicit generality (\"Reveals...Goodhart Collapse\") overpromises relative to a single-model study.\n\n- **W2: Small sample sizes limit statistical power at critical decision points.** The non-hacking rate comparison (ISR 6.06% vs. random 9.5%, p=0.182) is the paper's central null result -- ISR-as-reward does not improve structural quality over random. But with N=3 seeds and evaluation sets of 100 examples yielding ~5 non-hacking samples per condition, the study is severely underpowered to detect modest effects. The 5-seed extension (s45, s46 yielding ~55% non-hacking) demonstrates massive seed sensitivity, yet these additional seeds were added only for ISR beta=0.1, not for exec-only or random controls, creating an asymmetric comparison. More seeds for all conditions would substantially strengthen the claims.\n\n- **W3: The \"non-hacking\" rate is an upper bound with unknown tightness.** The paper explicitly states \"N-H rate is an upper bound on genuine reasoning: the residual matching none of the eight cataloged patterns (classifier recall is unknown).\" The PG mode was discovered only through manual audit of seed 42, and seeds 43/44 checkpoints were deleted before audit. This means the non-hacking rates for 2 of 3 ISR seeds may be overestimated, and the magnitude of overestimation is unknowable. For a paper whose central message depends on distinguishing hacking from non-hacking, this is a non-trivial gap. The 50-sample precision audit (>95%) is helpful but does not address recall.\n\n- **W4: The seed 43 evaluation anomaly is concerning.** Seed 43 was evaluated with temperature=0.7 sampling rather than greedy decoding due to \"greedy-mode degeneration.\" This introduces a confound: is the behavioral difference between s43 and other seeds a consequence of training dynamics or evaluation protocol? The sensitivity analysis (Appendix Table 12, excluding s43) is good practice, but the fact that qualitative conclusions are preserved without s43 also suggests s43 contributes noise rather than signal. The paper would be cleaner if s43 were either fully excluded or evaluated with a systematic protocol for handling greedy failures.\n\n- **W5: Exec-only results rely heavily on a single seed (s42).** Table 1 in the main text reports exec-only results for seed 42 only (PR=93%, CE=87%). Seed 43 has PR=13% (greedy failure) and seed 44 has 100% TS (a qualitatively different mode). The \"87% CE\" finding that opens the paper is seed-specific. While the cross-seed divergence is itself presented as a finding, this means the headline claim (\"93% prove rate, 87% embed the conclusion\") characterizes one seed, not the condition. The 1000-step convergence (all seeds >=93% hacking) is more robust but is based on N=3.\n\n- **W6: ISR computational cost during training is underreported.** The paper states STA adds <1% overhead during evaluation (5ms per solver call, ~55ms per sample vs. 4s for inference). But during training with ISR as reward, STA must be computed for every proved sample in every batch, across K=4 rollouts. With 500 training examples, 500 steps, K=4, and variable prove rates, the total number of solver calls during training could be substantial. The paper does not report wall-clock training time comparisons between exec-only and ISR conditions.\n\n- **W7: ProofFOL \"genuine\" uses a coarser classifier.** The paper notes that ProofFOL genuine rates exclude only TS and CE (text-match detectable), unlike FOLIO's eight-mode residual. This means the 76.8% vs. 17.4% comparison is not apples-to-apples, and the beneficial-instability claim is weaker than it first appears. The paper does flag this (\"not directly comparable\") but the framing in the Introduction (\"76.8% vs. 17.4% genuine\") buries this crucial caveat.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** Seeds 45 and 46 show ~55% non-hacking under ISR beta=0.1, dramatically different from the original 3 seeds (~6%). Were corresponding additional seeds run for exec-only and random conditions? If not, the bimodal outcome cannot be attributed to ISR specifically -- it may be a general GRPO phenomenon. What is the authors' hypothesis for why these two seeds behave differently?\n\n- **Q2:** The paper states \"s43/s44 checkpoints were deleted before audit\" for PG detection. Given that PG was discovered in the sole seed that was audited (s42, 5.2%), and that PG inflates ISR by construction, what is the authors' estimate of how much the non-hacking rate would change if PG were detectable across all seeds and conditions?\n\n- **Q3:** The shuffled-ISR control yields PR=15.3% with 87% non-hacking (n=46 proved). This is essentially identical to SFT (PR=13%, 92% non-hacking). Does this mean the ISR signal is doing nothing beyond what SFT achieves, and the multiplicative coupling simply makes it harder for the model to find proved formalizations?\n\n- **Q4:** The paper uses FOLIO (500 train, 100 eval) throughout. At this evaluation scale, non-hacking rates of ~5% correspond to ~5 samples. Have the authors considered evaluating on a larger held-out set, or are there principled reasons the FOLIO evaluation set is limited to 100?\n\n- **Q5:** For the Goodhart analysis: ISR converts CE into TS without reducing total hacking. But TS is arguably a more extreme form of degeneration (single component vs. embedded among multiple). Does the authors' framework distinguish degrees of hacking severity, or is all hacking treated as equivalent?\n\n- **Q6:** Qwen3-8B stagnated at PR=15.5% with K=4. Was K=8 (as mentioned in the task for 8B) actually tried? The task description references \"K=8 (8B)\" and Tables 5-6 for cross-scale results, but these do not appear in the paper.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The footnote distinguishing \"intensional\" from \"intentional\" (Section 3) is helpful but could be elevated to the main text since ISR is the central metric.\n- Table 2 (taxonomy table) uses \"---\" and seed identifiers inconsistently; some cells have \"s43\" indicating seed-specific modes while others have \"1k\" (presumably 1000-step specific). A more systematic encoding would improve readability.\n- The \"contradiction explosion\" mode (Appendix) is listed in Table 2 with \"1k\" but the appendix example shows it as a 1000-step phenomenon. It should be clearer that this mode was only observed at extended training, not the default 500 steps.\n- The title uses \"Structural Degeneration and Goodhart Effects\" which is accurate but the paper summary and earlier versions apparently used \"Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse\" -- the current title is more appropriate given the single-scale evidence.\n- Figure 1 caption references \"(proved samples only, rolling w=20)\" but the rolling window size may smooth over important transitions; were different window sizes explored?\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3.5/5.** The LOO methodology is well-motivated and correctly applied. The key limitation is that ISR measures component necessity rather than semantic quality, which the authors acknowledge. The controls (random, shuffled-ISR, SFT) are well-designed. The hacking taxonomy is reasonable but not exhaustively validated (recall unknown). The statistical framework is honest about its limitations. The premise-count deconfounding is a strong methodological contribution. Deductions are for: single model family, the unknown recall of the non-hacking classifier, and the deleted checkpoints preventing full PG audit.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5.** Hyperparameters are fully specified (Table in Appendix). Seeds, dataset sizes, solver versions, and evaluation protocols are documented. The seed 43 anomaly is disclosed. Code is promised (anonymized URL). The main gap is the ProofFOL construction pipeline (GPT-4o translation with ~70% retention) which would be difficult to exactly reproduce. Overall, the level of detail is sufficient for replication.\n\n- **Overall: 3.5/5.** This is a solid empirical case study that documents a real and important phenomenon (structural degeneration under GRPO with execution rewards, and Goodhart effects on structural diagnostics). The experimental design, with its multiple controls and deconfounding analyses, exceeds the typical standard for empirical ML papers. However, the single-model limitation, small sample sizes at critical decision points, asymmetric seed counts across conditions, and the unknowable tightness of the non-hacking upper bound prevent a confident \"4\" rating. The paper is at the boundary between main conference and Findings: the phenomenon is real and the methodology is careful, but the evidence base (one model family, 3-5 seeds, 100-example eval set) feels thin for the strength of the claims. With cross-model replication (even one additional model family showing convergent results) or substantially more seeds, this would be a clear main-conference paper. As-is, it is a strong Findings paper or a borderline main-conference accept (3.5 = Borderline Conference on the EMNLP scale)."}],"totalDurationMs":136829,"totalTokens":58482,"totalToolUseCount":12,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":982,"cache_read_input_tokens":54208,"output_tokens":3291,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":982},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3291,"cache_read_input_tokens":54208,"cache_creation_input_tokens":982,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":982,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
