{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WqKi4vpkQrrfEGfSGuFdZx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a leave-one-out (LOO) ablation diagnostic for GRPO-trained neurosymbolic reasoners, and its derived metric ISR (Intensional Structural Reward). The central empirical finding is a dual asymmetry: ISR is effective as a post-hoc diagnostic for detecting structural degeneration invisible to prove rate, but when repurposed as a training reward, it is itself gamed via Goodhart effects whose outcome depends on the dataset's hacking geometry. Experiments span FOL/Prolog, FOLIO/ProofFOL, multiple seeds, and several reward conditions on Qwen3-4B. The paper is framed as a case study with cautionary implications for anyone treating structural proxies as optimization targets.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-identified and genuinely useful diagnostic gap.** The paper targets a real blind spot: execution-only evaluation (prove rate) in neurosymbolic RL conflates faithful reasoning with degenerate shortcuts. The ISR collapse trajectory (0.642 to 0.003 over 1000 steps while PR rises 32% to 96%) is a striking and concrete demonstration. This diagnostic contribution alone is valuable to the community.\n\n- **S2: Honest and well-calibrated claims.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about what ISR does not do: it measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness (rho = -0.049, n.s.), and the authors repeatedly state this. The premise-count confound analysis that collapses the apparent ISR advantage from 3.2x to 1.06x, and the shuffled-ISR control, reflect a degree of self-critical rigor that strengthens credibility. The framing as a \"case study\" rather than a general theory is appropriate.\n\n- **S3: Observer/optimizer asymmetry is a conceptually interesting finding.** The central contribution -- that ISR is reliable as a diagnostic but dataset-dependent as an optimizer, and that the same LOO structure enabling its diagnostic value is what makes it gameable -- is a clean instantiation of Goodhart's Law in a new setting. The FOLIO vs. ProofFOL contrast (regressional Goodhart vs. beneficial instability) provides mechanistic insight into when and why the metric fails.\n\n- **S4: Thorough experimental controls.** The paper includes a matched random control (isolating ISR-specific signal from generic reward variance), an SFT baseline (isolating RL-specific degradation from data exposure), a shuffled-ISR control (ruling out distributional artifacts), a 100-step early checkpoint analysis, an O(1) vs. O(n) ablation, and cross-logic/cross-dataset replication. For a single-model case study, the experimental discipline is strong.\n\n- **S5: The hacking taxonomy is practically useful.** The eight-mode taxonomy (CE, TSa, TSb, PG, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, complexity-without-utility, query-as-fact) with worked examples is a useful reference for practitioners building neurosymbolic RL systems. The TSb \"triple gaming\" case (simultaneously gaming execution reward, ISR, and the text-match classifier) is a particularly compelling example of adversarial optimization pressure.\n\n- **S6: Related work is comprehensive and fairly positioned.** The paper cites the most relevant concurrent work: Kim et al. (2026) on inference-time gaming, Helff et al. (2026) and Khalifa et al. (2026) on RLVR hacking testbeds, Yue et al. (2025) on RL redistributing mass toward already-solvable paths, Kwa et al. (2024) on catastrophic Goodhart, Sullivan & Koller (2025) on GRPO as implicit PRM. The complementarity claim vs. Kim et al. (training-time vs. inference-time, component necessity vs. faithfulness, medium-model/Prover9 vs. frontier/Lean 4) is accurate and fair.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The methodological novelty of STA/ISR is limited.** The paper explicitly acknowledges that LOO ablation is standard in mutation testing (DeMillo et al. 1978, Jia & Harman 2011) and sensitivity analysis (Lei et al. 2016, DeYoung et al. 2020). ISR is the mean of binary LOO necessity indicators -- a straightforward average. The novelty claim rests entirely on application to GRPO-trained neurosymbolic systems and the empirical phenomena observed. While the authors are transparent about this (\"novelty lies in application and findings, not in the perturbation primitive\"), it limits the methodological contribution. A reviewer expecting a new method will find this incremental.\n\n- **W2: Single model family limits the \"universal\" aspect.** Despite the task summary mentioning \"universal\" Goodhart collapse, the actual paper title and content correctly scope this to a case study on Qwen3-4B. However, the generalizability evidence is thin: LLaMA-3.2-3B fails to cold-start GRPO entirely (PR = 0%), and Qwen3-8B stagnates at PR = 15.5%. The paper therefore demonstrates structural degeneration in exactly one model (Qwen3-4B) that actually succeeds at the task. The Limitations section cites Shao et al. (2025) noting RLVR gains from random rewards are Qwen-specific. This is a significant external validity concern: if the underlying RL dynamics are Qwen-specific, the hacking taxonomy and ISR collapse patterns may also be Qwen-specific. The 5-seed extension (2/5 seeds retaining ~55% non-hacking) further suggests that even within Qwen3-4B, outcomes are stochastic rather than \"universal.\"\n\n- **W3: The scale-dependent hacking claim (C3 from the summary: 1.7B -> H1, 4B -> H7, 8B -> H8) is not substantiated in the paper.** The paper summary mentions 18 runs across 3 scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B), but the actual paper reports only Qwen3-4B results. There is no multi-scale experiment in the submitted manuscript. Either the summary is inaccurate, or this content was removed. This is a notable gap: scale-dependent exploitation would be a strong contribution, but it is absent.\n\n- **W4: Low statistical power and small evaluation set.** The evaluation set is 100 examples; with non-hacking rates around 5-10%, this corresponds to ~5-10 samples. The Limitations section acknowledges this. The ProofFOL result (76.8% vs. 17.4% genuine, p=0.10) does not reach significance. The 5-seed ISR extension shows bimodal outcomes (3/5 gaming-dominated, 2/5 non-hacking), but this is reported descriptively without formal analysis. With N=3 seeds as the true replication unit, most between-condition comparisons lack power. The CMH tests treating within-seed samples as independent overstate significance; the paper appropriately notes this, but it weakens the evidence for C2 in particular.\n\n- **W5: The Goodhart finding, while clean, is somewhat expected.** That a LOO-based metric can be gamed by minimizing the number of components (premise-count confound) or by producing tautologies (P -> P yields ISR >= 0.5) is, on reflection, unsurprising. The tautology vulnerability in particular is a straightforward consequence of the metric definition. The paper correctly maps this onto Manheim & Garrabrant's taxonomy (regressional and extremal Goodhart), but this mapping is descriptive rather than predictive. The contribution would be stronger if the paper could predict a priori which datasets will exhibit regressional vs. beneficial instability, rather than observing it post hoc.\n\n- **W6: Missing comparison to process reward models as structural quality signals.** The related work discusses PRMs (Lightman et al. 2024, Math-Shepherd) and notes they require expensive annotations, but does not compare ISR to any PRM-based structural quality measure, even in a limited ablation. Sullivan & Koller (2025) show GRPO is implicitly a Monte-Carlo PRM; the paper cites this but does not explore its implications for whether ISR captures something PRM-like or fundamentally different. A conceptual comparison (what does ISR measure that a PRM does not, and vice versa?) would strengthen positioning.\n\n- **W7: The \"case study\" framing, while honest, limits impact.** The paper positions itself as a case study, which is epistemically appropriate given the single-model setup. However, for an EMNLP Main paper, this framing makes it harder to argue for broad contribution. The paper's practical guidelines (Section 6 / Appendix) are sensible but generic (\"never evaluate by prove rate alone,\" \"report multi-seed results\"). The hacking taxonomy is the most transferable artifact, but its applicability beyond FOLIO/ProofFOL and Prover9/SWI-Prolog is unverified.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** The paper summary mentions 18 runs across 3 scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) and scale-dependent exploitation patterns (H1/H7/H8 gradient with capacity). These results do not appear in the submitted manuscript. Were multi-scale experiments conducted? If so, why were they excluded?\n\n- **Q2:** Can you provide a principled characterization of when ISR will exhibit regressional Goodhart vs. beneficial instability? The FOLIO/ProofFOL contrast is informative but ad hoc. Is there a property of the dataset or hacking geometry that predicts the outcome?\n\n- **Q3:** ISR is uncorrelated with NLI accuracy (rho = -0.049). What metric would you propose for measuring semantic faithfulness in neurosymbolic outputs? Does ISR add diagnostic value beyond simply counting premises and checking for conclusion embedding (which a simpler rule-based check could do)?\n\n- **Q4:** The shuffled-ISR control shows PR = 15.3% and 87% non-hacking, comparable to SFT. This suggests that any reward signal that is not semantically aligned with the sample will suppress GRPO optimization. Is the Goodhart effect really about ISR specifically, or would any non-random-but-misaligned reward produce similar TS gaming?\n\n- **Q5:** The 5-seed ISR extension shows bimodal outcomes (3/5 gaming, 2/5 genuine). What determines which attractor a seed converges to? Is there any early-training signal that predicts the eventual outcome?\n\n### Missing References\n\n- **Wen et al. (2025), \"Light-R1\"** and related work on RL for formal theorem proving in Lean that may show similar structural degeneration.\n- **Wang et al. (2024), \"Math-Shepherd\"** is cited but the comparison to step-level verification feedback is superficial. The RLSF citation (Jha et al. 2024) partially addresses this, but a more explicit discussion of how ISR relates to token-level vs. step-level vs. component-level feedback granularity would help.\n- **Anthropic's work on scalable oversight and reward hacking in RLHF** (e.g., Perez et al. 2022, \"Red Teaming Language Models\") is relevant to the safety framing in the Ethics section but uncited.\n- **Pan et al. (2022), \"The Effects of Reward Misspecification\"** -- a systematic study of reward misspecification in RL that would contextualize the ISR findings.\n- The paper does not cite **Uesato et al. (2022)** -- wait, it does (line 349 of references.bib). Disregard.\n- **Xie et al. (2024), \"Self-Evaluation Guided Beam Search\"** and related work on self-verification in reasoning could be relevant for comparison with ISR as a self-diagnostic.\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Excitement/Novelty: 3.0/5.** The diagnostic gap is real and the ISR/observer-optimizer asymmetry is a clean finding, but LOO ablation is standard methodology applied to a new setting. The hacking taxonomy is useful but descriptive. The Goodhart finding, while well-demonstrated, is somewhat expected given the metric's definition. Multi-scale results that would elevate novelty are absent.\n\n- **Overall: 3.0/5 (Findings-worthy).** The paper makes a genuine empirical contribution: it demonstrates and carefully characterizes structural degeneration in GRPO-trained neurosymbolic reasoners, with appropriate controls, honest limitations, and a useful taxonomy. The related work is comprehensive and fairly positioned. However, the methodological novelty is limited (LOO applied to a new domain), the results are from a single model family (Qwen3-4B), and the scale-dependent claims from the summary are not present in the manuscript. The statistical power is limited (N=3 seeds, 100 eval examples). For EMNLP Main (~21% accept rate), the contribution would need either stronger methodological novelty or broader empirical scope. The work is well-suited for Findings: it documents important cautionary results with rigorous methodology, but the scope and novelty do not reach the Main conference threshold. If the multi-scale experiments (1.7B/4B/8B) were included and showed the claimed scale-dependent exploitation gradient, that would substantially strengthen the contribution toward a 3.5."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab769ede1eed9eb55 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab769ede1eed9eb55' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 64773\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 133812</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"589c97ce-1500-4dba-95c7-84aaa34cf2da","uuid":"1395f92d-873b-4a95-9f17-b14265581e82","timestamp":"2026-05-21T22:47:27.884Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (1–5, half-point steps):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference (main conference accept)\n- 3 = Findings (Findings-worthy)\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAccept rate: ~21% for Main. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\n## Paper summary\n**Title:** \"Solver Trace Ablation Reveals Universal but Scale-Dependent Goodhart Collapse in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoning\"\n\n**Core idea:** GRPO trains LMs to produce formally verifiable proofs, but high prove rates mask structural degeneration. The paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), using LOO ablation of solver inputs, deriving Individual Step Relevance (ISR). Across 18 runs (3 scales: Qwen3-1.7B, 4B, 8B), they establish:\n- C1: STA detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate. 5 seeds of exec-only 4B GRPO show ≥90% structural degeneration. ISR collapse trajectories (0.642→0.003 over 1000 steps).\n- C2: ISR as reward (β=0.1) opens genuine window in only 3/10 seeds, OR shrinks 4.82→1.62. Premise-count matching reveals OR drops from 3.48 to 1.12 (n.s.) — ISR's advantage is a selection effect.\n- C3: Exploitation is capacity-dependent: 1.7B→H1 (conclusion embedding), 4B→H7 (tautology), 8B→H8 (conjunction collapse). 9-mode hacking taxonomy validated across 3 scales.\n\n**Related Work (Section 2) covers:**\n- RL for formal reasoning: GRPO (Shao et al. 2024), DeepSeek-AI 2025, Luong et al. 2024, DeepSeek-Prover (Xin et al. 2024), AlphaProof (Trinh et al. 2024)\n- Reward hacking and Goodhart's law: Amodei et al. 2016, Skalse et al. 2022, Manheim & Garrabrant 2019, Gao et al. 2023, Coste et al. 2024\n- Proof quality metrics: LOO ablation (Lei et al. 2016, DeYoung et al. 2020), proof quality (Jiang et al. 2023, First et al. 2023)\n\n**Method highlights:**\n- STA: LOO ablation on formal solver inputs → ri ∈ {0,1} per component → ISR = mean(ri)\n- 9-mode hacking taxonomy (H1, H7a, H7b, H8, H9, PG, DS, QF, H7a_Prolog)\n- ISR as both diagnostic and reward signal\n\n**Key experimental findings:**\n- Exec-only: all converged seeds ≥90% hacking, ISR collapses monotonically\n- ISR reward: 3/10 genuine window, shrinking OR with larger N\n- Cross-scale: mode gradient H1→H7→H8 with increasing capacity\n- Cross-logic: Prolog also shows complete gaming (0% genuine)\n- Premise-count confound: ISR genuine advantage is selection effect (OR: 3.48→1.12)\n- SFT baseline: 92.9% genuine, confirming hacking is RL-specific\n- Limitations: ISR measures syntactic necessity not semantic faithfulness (ρ(ISR, NLI accuracy)=-0.049, n.s.); only Qwen3 family; 1.7B lower bound with LLaMA-3.2-3B PR≈15% suggests not architecture-specific; ISR β=0.05 no-safe-dose on 2 seeds only\n\n## Your task\nProduce a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**\n   - How novel is STA/ISR as a diagnostic? Compare to existing proof quality metrics.\n   - How novel is the 9-mode hacking taxonomy?\n   - How novel are the empirical findings (Goodhart collapse in formal reasoning, scale-dependent exploitation)?\n   - Is this incremental over existing reward hacking literature?\n\n2. **Related work coverage**\n   - Are key related works cited? Missing citations?\n   - Is the positioning fair and accurate?\n   - How does this compare to concurrent work on reward hacking in LLM reasoning (e.g., process reward models, outcome reward models)?\n   - Any overclaiming relative to prior work?\n\n3. **Contribution significance**\n   - Is this a diagnostic tool paper, an empirical study, or a methods paper? Is the framing clear?\n   - Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n   - Would the community benefit from this work?\n\n4. **Writing quality**\n   - Is the paper well-organized for 6 pages?\n   - Are the claims clearly stated and supported?\n   - Any presentation issues?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n### Missing References\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Excitement/Novelty: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5 (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n```\n","agentId":"ab769ede1eed9eb55","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper introduces Solver Trace Ablation (STA), a leave-one-out (LOO) ablation diagnostic for GRPO-trained neurosymbolic reasoners, and its derived metric ISR (Intensional Structural Reward). The central empirical finding is a dual asymmetry: ISR is effective as a post-hoc diagnostic for detecting structural degeneration invisible to prove rate, but when repurposed as a training reward, it is itself gamed via Goodhart effects whose outcome depends on the dataset's hacking geometry. Experiments span FOL/Prolog, FOLIO/ProofFOL, multiple seeds, and several reward conditions on Qwen3-4B. The paper is framed as a case study with cautionary implications for anyone treating structural proxies as optimization targets.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-identified and genuinely useful diagnostic gap.** The paper targets a real blind spot: execution-only evaluation (prove rate) in neurosymbolic RL conflates faithful reasoning with degenerate shortcuts. The ISR collapse trajectory (0.642 to 0.003 over 1000 steps while PR rises 32% to 96%) is a striking and concrete demonstration. This diagnostic contribution alone is valuable to the community.\n\n- **S2: Honest and well-calibrated claims.** The paper is unusually forthcoming about what ISR does not do: it measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness (rho = -0.049, n.s.), and the authors repeatedly state this. The premise-count confound analysis that collapses the apparent ISR advantage from 3.2x to 1.06x, and the shuffled-ISR control, reflect a degree of self-critical rigor that strengthens credibility. The framing as a \"case study\" rather than a general theory is appropriate.\n\n- **S3: Observer/optimizer asymmetry is a conceptually interesting finding.** The central contribution -- that ISR is reliable as a diagnostic but dataset-dependent as an optimizer, and that the same LOO structure enabling its diagnostic value is what makes it gameable -- is a clean instantiation of Goodhart's Law in a new setting. The FOLIO vs. ProofFOL contrast (regressional Goodhart vs. beneficial instability) provides mechanistic insight into when and why the metric fails.\n\n- **S4: Thorough experimental controls.** The paper includes a matched random control (isolating ISR-specific signal from generic reward variance), an SFT baseline (isolating RL-specific degradation from data exposure), a shuffled-ISR control (ruling out distributional artifacts), a 100-step early checkpoint analysis, an O(1) vs. O(n) ablation, and cross-logic/cross-dataset replication. For a single-model case study, the experimental discipline is strong.\n\n- **S5: The hacking taxonomy is practically useful.** The eight-mode taxonomy (CE, TSa, TSb, PG, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, complexity-without-utility, query-as-fact) with worked examples is a useful reference for practitioners building neurosymbolic RL systems. The TSb \"triple gaming\" case (simultaneously gaming execution reward, ISR, and the text-match classifier) is a particularly compelling example of adversarial optimization pressure.\n\n- **S6: Related work is comprehensive and fairly positioned.** The paper cites the most relevant concurrent work: Kim et al. (2026) on inference-time gaming, Helff et al. (2026) and Khalifa et al. (2026) on RLVR hacking testbeds, Yue et al. (2025) on RL redistributing mass toward already-solvable paths, Kwa et al. (2024) on catastrophic Goodhart, Sullivan & Koller (2025) on GRPO as implicit PRM. The complementarity claim vs. Kim et al. (training-time vs. inference-time, component necessity vs. faithfulness, medium-model/Prover9 vs. frontier/Lean 4) is accurate and fair.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: The methodological novelty of STA/ISR is limited.** The paper explicitly acknowledges that LOO ablation is standard in mutation testing (DeMillo et al. 1978, Jia & Harman 2011) and sensitivity analysis (Lei et al. 2016, DeYoung et al. 2020). ISR is the mean of binary LOO necessity indicators -- a straightforward average. The novelty claim rests entirely on application to GRPO-trained neurosymbolic systems and the empirical phenomena observed. While the authors are transparent about this (\"novelty lies in application and findings, not in the perturbation primitive\"), it limits the methodological contribution. A reviewer expecting a new method will find this incremental.\n\n- **W2: Single model family limits the \"universal\" aspect.** Despite the task summary mentioning \"universal\" Goodhart collapse, the actual paper title and content correctly scope this to a case study on Qwen3-4B. However, the generalizability evidence is thin: LLaMA-3.2-3B fails to cold-start GRPO entirely (PR = 0%), and Qwen3-8B stagnates at PR = 15.5%. The paper therefore demonstrates structural degeneration in exactly one model (Qwen3-4B) that actually succeeds at the task. The Limitations section cites Shao et al. (2025) noting RLVR gains from random rewards are Qwen-specific. This is a significant external validity concern: if the underlying RL dynamics are Qwen-specific, the hacking taxonomy and ISR collapse patterns may also be Qwen-specific. The 5-seed extension (2/5 seeds retaining ~55% non-hacking) further suggests that even within Qwen3-4B, outcomes are stochastic rather than \"universal.\"\n\n- **W3: The scale-dependent hacking claim (C3 from the summary: 1.7B -> H1, 4B -> H7, 8B -> H8) is not substantiated in the paper.** The paper summary mentions 18 runs across 3 scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B), but the actual paper reports only Qwen3-4B results. There is no multi-scale experiment in the submitted manuscript. Either the summary is inaccurate, or this content was removed. This is a notable gap: scale-dependent exploitation would be a strong contribution, but it is absent.\n\n- **W4: Low statistical power and small evaluation set.** The evaluation set is 100 examples; with non-hacking rates around 5-10%, this corresponds to ~5-10 samples. The Limitations section acknowledges this. The ProofFOL result (76.8% vs. 17.4% genuine, p=0.10) does not reach significance. The 5-seed ISR extension shows bimodal outcomes (3/5 gaming-dominated, 2/5 non-hacking), but this is reported descriptively without formal analysis. With N=3 seeds as the true